    "collected_at": "2026-11-25T16:17:00.503102+00:00",
    "origin_id": "https://www.supplychain247.com/article/us-canada-tariff-changes-importer-compliance",
    "canonical_url": "https://www.supplychain247.com/article/us-canada-tariff-changes-importer-compliance",
    "origin_publisher": "Supply Chain 24/7",
    "published_at": "2026-11-25T07:04:00-05:00",
    "published_at_raw": "2026-11-25T07:04:00-05:00",
    "publication_precision": "TIMESTAMP",
    "acquired_at": "2026-11-25T16:18:48.806320+00:00",
    "available_at": "2026-11-25T16:18:48.806320+00:00",
    "body": "Changes to U.S. tariffs on Canadian goods took effect October 30, adding 100 product classifications to the list subject to a 50% additional tariff and removing 22. Newly covered products include certain valves, carpets, waste products and cocoa powder. Products removed from the list include certain plywood sheets, paper products, machinery, refrigeration equipment and packaging materials.\nWhat’s Related\nThe October 23 presidential proclamation revised which products face the additional tariff under Section 338 of the Tariff Act of 1930. The changes apply to goods entered for consumption or withdrawn from a warehouse for consumption on or after October 30. The additional tariff applies on top of any applicable Section 232 duties.\nOn November 14, Canadian Prime Minister Mark Carney said the countries were making progress in trade talks, though no agreement had been reached. He said protecting Canada’s automotive industry remained a priority. With talks still underway, importers need to know which products are affected now, whether their customs filings reflect the changes, and what the revised duties mean for their costs.\nStart with product classification\nA product’s code in the Harmonized Tariff Schedule of the United States (HTSUS) helps determine what duties an importer owes. A change in tariff coverage can raise or lower the cost of importing a product even when its classification stays the same.\nImporters should match the revised list against the codes they use for goods sourced from Canada. Products with broad descriptions or incomplete specifications deserve a closer look. A precise description of what a product is made of and how it’s used can help teams confirm the right code. When a classification is uncertain, current customs guidance and rulings can help resolve the question.\nCheck origin and customs records\nA product shipped from Canada may have a different country of origin for customs purposes. That distinction matters when components move between the United States, Canada, and Mexico during manufacturing . Importers should keep supplier records and manufacturing details that show how they determined the product’s origin.\nEach tariff measure has its own rules for how it interacts with trade agreements. The additional Section 338 tariff applies to covered products even when they qualify for preferential treatment under the United States-Mexico-Canada Agreement (USMCA).\nCommercial invoices, packing lists, bills of lading, and origin records should match the information used in customs declarations. A vague invoice description or an outdated classification in a product record can make it harder to confirm what duty applies. For companies with thousands of items and multiple suppliers, keeping product details in one place makes it easier to find affected items and give customs brokers the information they need.\nWork out what the changes will cost\nOnce importers identify affected products, they can recalculate landed costs. That means accounting for applicable duties and other charges, as well as any quotas, exclusions, or exemptions that affect the total. The results can inform purchasing decisions, inventory plans, and customer pricing.\nAn importer whose refrigeration equipment falls under one of the removed codes may pay less to bring that equipment into the United States. An importer whose valves fall under one of the newly covered codes may face a substantial increase. The financial impact depends on each item's classification and which duties apply.\nConnecting tariff changes to product records, purchase orders, and shipments gives leaders a clearer picture of how much they may owe and when. That timing matters if a company needs to adjust prices, review a supplier agreement, or make a purchasing decision before its next shipment arrives.\nMake tariff monitoring part of the routine\nThe October 23 proclamation gave importers one week before the changes took effect. U.S. Customs and Border Protection issued filing guidance identifying affected classifications and explaining how to handle entries. A company that reviews tariffs only during scheduled compliance audits could miss changes that affect shipments already in transit.\nImporters need a process to check government announcements, match affected tariff codes to their products and update duty calculations and filing instructions. Automated tools can flag products for review across a large catalog. Customs specialists still need to look at cases where a product’s classification or origin is unclear, or where the new rules leave room for interpretation.\nTeams also need a way to get changes to the people who act on them. A customs broker may need revised filing instructions for an upcoming entry, while a buyer may need an updated cost before placing the next order. Both decisions depend on having current information about the product and the shipment.\nPlan around today’s rules\nCompanies should keep watching for announcements from both governments as trade talks continue. Until a change formally takes effect, importers need to plan around the rules that apply to their shipments today. With current product records and a regular review process, they can see sooner which goods are affected, how much costs could change, and where an entry needs a closer look.\nShannon Hynds is CEO of Quickcode.",
